          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"“Moment” is not a fixed biblical symbol; contextually it denotes brevity, transience, or sudden divine action, including sudden judgment and resurrection transformation.\",\"book_summary\":\"Brief prophetic time expressions point directly or typologically to one appointed eschatological moment: Babylon the Great’s sudden destruction, coordinated with resurrection, gathering, and protection through judgment.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A short interval emphasizing brevity or suddenness, especially in divine judgment or eschatological transformation.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A prophetic short-time marker for a specific coordinated end-time event centered on Babylon’s sudden destruction and the saints’ resurrection and preservation.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus treats short-time expressions as context-dependent and allows multiple distinct events and themes. The book restricts their prophetic significance toward one appointed eschatological complex and reclassifies Babylon’s fall, resurrection, gathering, and protection—and potentially typological short intervals—as manifestations of that moment.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction in identifying “moment” with sudden judgment or resurrection, both of which appear in the consensus. The material disagreement is the book’s unification of contextually distinct short intervals into a single prophetic referent, which the consensus does not recognize as settled.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus category of sudden eschatological divine action but gives it a much more specific referent and typological reach. Its core is therefore recognizable rather than novel, yet it materially narrows and reorganizes the cases covered, making REFINED more appropriate than MATCH or DIVERGENT.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed texts independently support sudden judgment on Babylon and instantaneous resurrection, but they do not by themselves establish that these occur as one coordinated “moment,” nor do they explicitly supply the gathering, protection-through-judgment, or general type-shadow-similitude rule.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
